# Keep secrets, history and test material out of the build context and image.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
# TLS material never enters a build context, so it can never be baked into an
# image layer — where it would survive every `docker rmi` of the running
# container and be readable by anyone who can pull the image (ADR-026 §4).
deploy/certs
deploy/secrets
prod.env
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.p12
*.pfx
.git
.github
.venv
venv

tests
docs
# The Run 3 fault-injection policy (ADR-035, Fixture B) must not exist inside a
# shipped image. `COPY config ./config` is unfiltered, so without this line the
# file would ship in the production image and production would be one
# environment variable — FIREWALL_POLICY_FILE — away from loading a policy whose
# whole purpose is to stall the detector. `compose.fault.yaml` bind-mounts it at
# runtime instead, which is why the fixture is opt-in at the mount rather than at
# the setting. Asserted by tests/security/test_fault_fixtures_are_not_production.py.
config/policies/fault-injection.yaml
# Evaluation artefacts: ship EXACTLY the files the dashboard opens and nothing
# else. `app/api/dashboard/evaluations.py` reads one filename — `result.json`,
# one level under eval/results — so that is the whole allow-list.
#
# It was previously an exclude-list naming `predictions*`, `*.csv` and `*.svg`.
# That drifted the moment Phase 15's benchmark harness started writing
# `raw_results.jsonl`, a name none of those patterns match: 17 MB of per-request
# benchmark rows shipped in the production image while this comment claimed
# ~200K. An allow-list cannot drift that way — a new artefact type is excluded by
# default instead of included by omission (ADR-032).
eval
!eval/results/*/result.json
scripts

**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache
.mypy_cache
.ruff_cache
.coverage
coverage.xml
htmlcov

models
*.md
!README.md
.idea
.vscode
.DS_Store
artifacts
